<body>

<h1 class="page-title">Why Is the Sky Blue? | 天空为什么是蓝色的？</h1>
<p class="page-subtitle">A bilingual exploration across three levels of understanding | 三个认知水平的双语探索</p>

<div class="container">
  <!-- English Column -->
  <div class="column" id="english-col">
    <div class="column-header">
      <span class="lang-badge">EN</span>
      <h2>English</h2>
    </div>

    <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?</h1>

    <h3><span class="section-icon">🧒</span> For a 3-Year-Old</h3>
    <div class="toddler-text">
      <p>You know how sunlight looks white? Well, it's actually made of lots of colors — like a rainbow hiding inside! When sunlight comes down to Earth, it hits the air all around us. The tiny bits of air play a game with the light, and they bounce the blue color everywhere — up, down, sideways, all over! So when you look up, your eyes catch all that bounced-around blue light. That's why the sky looks blue!</p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h3><span class="section-icon">🎓</span> For a High School Student</h3>
    <div class="highschool-text">
      <p>Sunlight appears white but is actually a mixture of all visible wavelengths (roughly 380–700 nm). When this light enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with molecules of nitrogen and oxygen that are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light. This triggers a phenomenon called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>, where the intensity of scattered light is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength:</p>
      <div class="math-block">I ∝ 1 / λ⁴</div>
      <p>This means shorter wavelengths (blue/violet, ~400–450 nm) are scattered roughly 5–10 times more strongly than longer wavelengths (red, ~650 nm). Blue light gets scattered in all directions by atmospheric molecules, so no matter where you look in the sky, scattered blue light reaches your eyes.</p>
      <p>You might wonder: "If violet is even shorter than blue, why isn't the sky violet?" Two reasons — (1) the Sun emits less violet light than blue, and (2) human eyes are less sensitive to violet. Our combined cone responses make the scattered light appear blue.</p>
      <p>This also explains why sunsets are red: at low solar angles, light travels a longer path through the atmosphere, so most of the blue light is scattered <em>away</em> before reaching your eyes, leaving the longer red wavelengths to dominate.</p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h3><span class="section-icon">🔬</span> For a Physics PhD</h3>
    <div class="phd-text">
      <p>The blue color of the sky is a consequence of <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong> in a dilute, isotropic dielectric medium. For particles with size parameter <span class="math-inline">x = 2πr / λ ≪ 1</span>, the differential scattering cross-section for unpolarized light is:</p>
      <div class="math-block">dσ/dΩ = ½ (2π/λ)⁴ [(n²−1)/(n²+2)]² (3Nα/2)² V² (1 + cos²θ)</div>
      <p>where <span class="math-inline">n</span> is the refractive index of the scatterer, <span class="math-inline">N</span> is the number density, <span class="math-inline">α</span> is the polarizability, <span class="math-inline">V</span> is the particle volume, and <span class="math-inline">θ</span> is the scattering angle. The <span class="math-inline">λ⁻⁴</span> dependence follows directly from the induced dipole radiation pattern of a point polarizability driven by an oscillating field.</p>
      <p>The total scattering coefficient for dry air at STP at <span class="math-inline">λ = 550</span> nm is approximately <span class="math-inline">β ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻⁵</span> m⁻¹, giving a scattering mean free path of ~90 km — consistent with the optical thickness of the atmosphere being sufficient for single-scattering dominance in the visible.</p>
      <p>Several subtleties modulate the naive <span class="math-inline">λ⁻⁴</span> picture:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Molecular density fluctuations:</strong> Einstein–Smoluchowski theory shows that the relevant scatterers are not individual molecules but thermodynamic density fluctuations in a volume ~λ³. This correctly predicts the depolarization ratio observed experimentally (Cabanard et al.), which deviates from the Rayleigh prediction for pure polarizable spheres due to the anisotropy of molecular polarizability tensors (N₂, O₂ have significant anisotropy γ).</li>
        <li><strong>Aerosol and Mie contributions:</strong> In practice, the Ångström exponent <span class="math-inline">α_A</span> in <span class="math-inline">β ∝ λ⁻ᵅ</span> deviates from 4 as aerosol loading increases, shifting the sky toward white/pale blue.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple scattering and ozone:</strong> The Chappuis absorption band of O₃ (centered ~600 nm, weak but non-negligible over long slant paths) suppresses red in the zenith sky, and the Huggins bands in the near-UV reduce violet contribution, shifting perceived hue further toward blue.</li>
        <li><strong>Polarization pattern:</strong> Single Rayleigh scattering produces maximum polarization at <span class="math-inline">θ = 90°</span> from the solar disk, yielding the familiar Rayleigh sky polarization pattern used by bees and in Rayleigh-lidar remote sensing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The observed zenith sky chromaticity (CIE coordinates ≈ 0.17, 0.18 for a clear zenith at mid-latitudes) is well reproduced by combining the solar spectral irradiance (AM0 or AM1.5 depending on geometry), the molecular scattering phase function, the solar zenith angle-dependent path length, and the CIE 1931 color matching functions — confirming that a first-principles radiative transfer calculation with only molecular scatterers recovers the qualitative and quantitative hue.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Chinese Column -->
  <div class="column" id="chinese-col">
    <div class="column-header">
      <span class="lang-badge">中文</span>
      <h2>简体中文</h2>
    </div>

    <h1>天空为什么是蓝色的？</h1>

    <h3><span class="section-icon">🧒</span> 写给3岁的小朋友</h3>
    <div class="toddler-text">
      <p>你知道阳光看起来是白色的吗？其实呀，它里面藏着好多好多的颜色——就像一道彩虹躲在里面！当阳光照到地球上时，它会遇到我们周围的空气。空气里的小小颗粒会和光线做游戏，把蓝色的光弹得到处都是——上上下下、左左右右，到处都是！所以当你抬起头来看天空时，你的眼睛就会捕捉到那些被弹来弹去的蓝光。这就是天空看起来是蓝色的原因啦！</p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h3><span class="section-icon">🎓</span> 写给高中生</h3>
    <div class="highschool-text">
      <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但它实际上是所有可见光波长（大约380–700纳米）的混合。当光进入地球大气层时，它会与远小于可见光波长的氮气和氧气分子发生相互作用。这会引发一种叫做<strong>瑞利散射</strong>的现象，其中散射光的强度与波长的四次方成反比：</p>
      <div class="math-block">I ∝ 1 / λ⁴</div>
      <p>这意味着较短的波长（蓝光/紫光，约400–450纳米）比长波长（红光，约650纳米）的散射强度高大约5到10倍。蓝光被大气分子向四面八方散射，所以无论你朝天空的哪个方向看，散射的蓝光都会到达你的眼睛。</p>
      <p>你可能会想："如果紫色光比蓝色光的波长还短，为什么天空不是紫色的呢？"有两个原因——（1）太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少；（2）人眼对紫光的敏感度较低。我们锥体细胞的综合响应使散射光看起来是蓝色的。</p>
      <p>这也解释了为什么日落是红色的：当太阳角度很低时，光在大气中经过的路径更长，所以大部分蓝光在到达你的眼睛之前就已经被散射<em>走</em>了，只剩下较长的红色波长占主导地位。</p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h3><span class="section-icon">🔬</span> 写给物理学博士</h3>
    <div class="phd-text">
      <p>天空的蓝色是稀薄各向同性介质中<strong>瑞利散射</strong>的结果。对于尺度参数 <span class="math-inline">x = 2πr / λ ≪ 1</span> 的粒子，非偏振光的微分散射截面为：</p>
      <div class="math-block">dσ/dΩ = ½ (2π/λ)⁴ [(n²−1)/(n²+2)]² (3Nα/2)² V² (1 + cos²θ)</div>
      <p>其中 <span class="math-inline">n</span> 是散射体的折射率，<span class="math-inline">N</span> 是数密度，<span class="math-inline">α</span> 是极化率，<span class="math-inline">V</span> 是粒子体积，<span class="math-inline">θ</span> 是散射角。<span class="math-inline">λ⁻⁴</span> 依赖关系直接来源于振荡场驱动的点极化率所形成的诱导偶极子辐射方向图。</p>
      <p>标准温度和压强下干燥空气在 <span class="math-inline">λ = 550</span> nm 处的总散射系数约为 <span class="math-inline">β ≈ 1.1 × 10⁻⁵</span> m⁻¹，对应的散射平均自由程约为90公里——这与大气的光学厚度足以使单次散射在可见光波段占主导地位相一致。</p>
      <p>有几个微妙之处修正了朴素的 <span class="math-inline">λ⁻⁴</span> 图像：</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>分子密度涨落：</strong>爱因斯坦–斯莫卢霍夫斯基理论表明，相关散射体不是单个分子，而是体积约为 ~λ³ 内的热力学密度涨落。这正确地预测了实验中观测到的退偏振比（Cabanard等人），该值偏离了纯可极化球体的瑞利理论预测，原因是分子极化率张量的各向异性（N₂、O₂ 具有显著的各向异性 γ）。</li>
        <li><strong>气溶胶与米氏散射贡献：</strong>在实际中，<span class="math-inline">β ∝ λ⁻ᵅ</span> 中的 Ångström 指数 <span class="math-inline">α_A</span> 会随着气溶胶负载的增加而偏离4，使天空趋向于白色/浅蓝色。</li>
        <li><strong>多重散射与臭氧：</strong>O₃ 的 Chappuis 吸收带（中心约600 nm，虽然弱但在长斜程路径上不可忽略）会抑制天顶天空中的红色成分，近紫外区的 Huggins 带会减少紫色贡献，从而使感知色调进一步向蓝色偏移。</li>
        <li><strong>偏振模式：</strong>单次瑞利散射在距太阳圆盘 <span class="math-inline">θ = 90°</span> 处产生最大偏振度，形成蜜蜂导航和瑞利激光雷达遥感中所利用的经典瑞利天空偏振分布模式。</li>
      </ul>
      <p>观测到的天顶天空色度坐标（中纬度晴空天顶的CIE坐标约为 0.17, 0.18）可以通过结合太阳光谱辐照度（根据几何条件选择AM0或AM1.5）、分子散射相函数、随太阳天顶角变化的路径长度以及CIE 1931颜色匹配函数来很好地复现——这证实了仅使用分子散射体从第一性原理出发进行辐射传输计算即可恢复色调的定性和定量特征。</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Navigation buttons -->
<div class="toc">
  <button onclick="scrollToSection(0)" title="Toddler level">🧒</button>
  <button onclick="scrollToSection(1)" title="High school level">🎓</button>
  <button onclick="scrollToSection(2)" title="PhD level">🔬</button>
  <button onclick="scrollToTop()" title="Scroll to top">⬆</button>
</div>

<script>
function scrollToSection(index) {
  const sections = document.querySelectorAll('h3');
  const englishCol = document.getElementById('english-col');
  const chineseCol = document.getElementById('chinese-col');
  if (sections[index]) {
    const offset = sections[index].offsetTop - 60;
    englishCol.scrollTo({ top: offset, behavior: 'smooth' });
    chineseCol.scrollTo({ top: offset, behavior: 'smooth' });
  }
}

function scrollToTop() {
  const englishCol = document.getElementById('english-col');
  const chineseCol = document.getElementById('chinese-col');
  englishCol.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' });
  chineseCol.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' });
}

// Synchronized scrolling between columns
let syncing = false;
const englishCol = document.getElementById('english-col');
const chineseCol = document.getElementById('chinese-col');

englishCol.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  if (syncing) return;
  syncing = true;
  const ratio = englishCol.scrollTop / (englishCol.scrollHeight - englishCol.clientHeight);
  chineseCol.scrollTop = ratio * (chineseCol.scrollHeight - chineseCol.clientHeight);
  requestAnimationFrame(() => { syncing = false; });
});

chineseCol.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  if (syncing) return;
  syncing = true;
  const ratio = chineseCol.scrollTop / (chineseCol.scrollHeight - chineseCol.clientHeight);
  englishCol.scrollTop = ratio * (englishCol.scrollHeight - englishCol.clientHeight);
  requestAnimationFrame(() => { syncing = false; });
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
